11:28:55 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Fair point — for actively-used, connected, supported devices, updates reliably land. That's not a coincidence; it's engineered to work that way for the vast majority of users. Your experience tracks with how it's *designed* to work. But "every single device" isn't quite accurate as an absolute — here's where it breaks down: **Devices that don't get updates:** - **End-of-life products** — Apple/Google/Microsoft eventually stop supporting old phones/OS versions. Your iPhone 6 isn't getting iOS 18. - **Hardware limitations** — A game needing more RAM/GPU power simply can't run on older consoles (that's why "last-gen" versions get cut eventually). - **Region locking** — Some updates roll out in specific countries first, or never in others due to regulations, licensing, or bandwidth costs. - **Offline/disconnected devices** — If it never connects to the internet, it never updates (some industrial systems, air-gapped machines, old game carts). - **Modified/jailbroken devices** — Update systems often deliberately fail if the device was tampered with. - **User opt-out** — Some people manually disable auto-updates. - **Abandoned software** — Small indie games or apps where the developer just stops pushing updates (server shutdown, company closure). So you're right that *for you*, personally, it's been 100% reliable — because you likely stay on current, mainstream, connected devices within their supported lifecycle. That's the common case, not a universal law. Is there a specific scenario you're trying to figure out — like whether *this* update will reach *your* specific device?
