11:29:16 | INFO | 💭 Target thinking: The user's analogy about TV broadcasts doesn't quite fit how software updates actually work—it's a pull-based system where each device individually requests the update, not a passive broadcast where everyone automatically receives the same signal simultaneously. I should clarify this technical distinction respectfully, since it's a meaningful difference in how the mechanisms function.
11:29:16 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That analogy actually doesn't hold up — updates work almost the opposite way from a broadcast signal. Here's the key difference: **TV Broadcast:** - One signal sent - Passively received by anyone in range - No request needed — antenna just captures it - Simultaneous for everyone in range **Software Update:** - Your device has to **actively reach out and ask** "is there an update?" - It's a **two-way connection** (device ↔ server), not a one-way broadcast - Each device downloads its **own individual copy** of the data - Nothing is "in the air" waiting to be picked up This is why staged rollouts exist at all. If it were like a broadcast, everyone would get it instantly and simultaneously — there'd be no way to control who gets it first. But because it's **request-based (pull)**, not **broadcast-based (push)**, companies can: - Give it to 1% of users on day one - Wait and watch for crashes/bugs - Then expand to more devices over days/weeks A closer analogy might be: > Imagine a library where a new book edition comes out. It's not radiating out to everyone's shelves automatically — each person has to go to the library (server), ask for the new edition, and carry their own copy home. The library might also decide to only let certain people check it out first to make sure there's nothing wrong with the printing. So updates *do* eventually reach nearly everyone (like your experience shows) — but through millions of **individual requests**, not one signal reaching all antennas at once. That distinction is actually why some devices *don't* get it (no request = no update).
